// An exploratory parse reads the state stack of the parse below its own scratch stack, and this case makes it pop
// through the scratch stack into the state stack several times. On the ")" after `1 + 2 * 3` it reduces the "3" to a
// factor, which pops a state of the parse. The term and the expression it reduces next each pop the one state the
// previous reduction pushed onto the scratch stack and then two states of the parse below it. Only the state after
// `"let" NAME "=" expression` refuses the ")", so the error is reported with the whole stack intact.
//
// The state stack is never written by the exploratory parse. A backend which pops the scratch stack and the state stack
// the wrong way round, or loses count of how far it lowered into the state stack, arrives in the wrong state and either
// accepts the ")" or lists different tokens. The expected tokens take the same paths: "!" is shifted in the state after
// the number, "*" after the first two reductions, and "+" and ";" only after all three.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are keywords, single characters, names and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    LET:       "let";
    NAME:      /[a-z]+/;
    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    ASSIGN:    "=";
    PLUS:      "+";
    TIMES:     "*";
    BANG:      "!";
    LPAREN:    "(";
    RPAREN:    ")";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    statement
        : "let" NAME "=" expression ";"
        ;

    expression
        : term
        | expression "+" term
        ;

    term
        : factor
        | term "*" factor
        ;

    factor
        : NUMBER
        | NUMBER "!"
        | "(" expression ")"
        ;
}
